## Why This Is Asked
This question tests advanced tree DP with edge deletions and a non-trivial objective—maximizing the sum of component maxima. It requires careful state design, knapsack-style merging, and the ability to reconstruct the solution from computed values.

## Key Concepts
- **Tree DP with edge cuts**: Model each edge cut as separating a subtree into a new component
- **Knapsack-like merging over children**: Combine subproblems using convolution-style DP
- **Maintaining component maxima**: Track both the sum (dp) and the current component's max weight (mx)

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(n, edges, weights, k) {
    const children = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    for (const [u, v] of edges) children[u - 1].push(v - 1);

    const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const mx = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);

    function dfs(u) {
        dp[u][0] = 0;
        mx[u][0] = weights[u];
        for (const v of children[u]) {
            parent[v] = u;
            dfs(v);
            for (let t = k; t >= 0; t--) {
                const ndp = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                const nmx = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                for (let t1 = 0; t1 <= t; t1++) {
                    const t2 = t - t1;
                    // cut edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2] + mx[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], mx[u][t1]);
                    // keep edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], Math.max(mx[u][t1], mx[v][t2]));
                }
                dp[u] = ndp;
                mx[u] = nmx;
            }
        }
    }

    dfs(0);
    return { maxSum: dp[0][k], edgesToCut: [] }; // reconstruct edges by backtracking decisions
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Forgetting to reset mx when cutting**: When an edge is cut, the parent component's max weight remains unchanged
- **Incorrect knapsack direction**: Always iterate cuts in descending order to avoid reusing the same child multiple times
- **Off-by-one in component counting**: k edge cuts always produce k+1 components

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

